## SCOPE (disclosed)

* **(a) Representations.** `T_p HS²` is `ℝ^{1,2}/ℝp`, the tangent plane of the ray space at
  `[p]` (canonical up to the scaling of `p`, which does not move rays; the sup-norm sphere
  `GX.HS 2` carries no smooth structure). `ℝP¹` is the circle through `[z] ↦ z/z̄`; `ℝP̃¹ = ℝ`
  through `t ↦ [cos πt : sin πt]`, so BBS's "fixed homeomorphism" (l.676) is the identity.
* **(b) `G̃`** is the paper's "group of projective transformations of `ℝP̃¹`" (l.679–680). That it
  is the universal covering *group* of `PSL(2,ℝ)` (a topological statement) is not formalized:
  neither group carries a topology here.
* **(c) Normalisations (questions).** `angle δ = 2π`, so `ℝP¹` has angle `2π`. "The angle" is
  not normalised at l.744, but the paper fixes this normalisation through Lemma surgery-key
  (l.2952–2969, with `𝒫_θ` at l.2827, the massive particle of cone angle θ of l.1142–1159), as does NOTES-3+1 N4. The double-cover structure of
  (e) then gives the link of a regular point the angle `4π` (`GX.hasRealAngle_link`). At a
  time-like `p` only, the visual map `ℓ(p) → ∂ℍ²₊ ≅ ℝP¹` is a second, degree-one `ℝP¹`-structure,
  under which a link's angle is its cone angle; it does not exist at space-like or light-like `p`.
  The orientation of `HS²` is that of the unit sphere of `ℝ^{1,2}`, outward normal first
  (`GX.vol3`), and `ℝP¹` is oriented counterclockwise. This is a convention only. The opposite
  orientation of `HS²` (or of `ℝP̃¹`) gives the mirror `ℝP¹`-structure; the canonical orientation
  (l.729–731) flips with it. Its lifted holonomy is `c̃ f⁻¹ c̃ = δᵏ (c̃ g⁻¹ c̃)` (`c̃ x = -x`) and
  its `I^±` are `-I^±`. Since `g'(-x) ≥ -x` iff `g x ≥ x`, the degree, the trace class, the
  angle and the sign (positive or negative, l.908–922) are all unchanged (not formalized).
  "Elliptic" `ℝP¹`-circles include those with central holonomy (l.693–694):
  `pos_of_isElliptic` takes "elliptic or `1`".
* **(d) Not formalized** (prose, no label): the classification of `ℝP¹`-circles (l.701–760) and
  "elliptic elements act as translations" (conjugacy to a translation; only freeness and proper
  discontinuity are proved); the parametrisation by the trace; the developing map of a
  `(Γ_p, ℓ(p))`-circle (needs `(Γ_p, ℓ(p))` analytic, not proved); `c_v`, `𝔢(L)`, `i^±`.
* **(e) Reading of l.770.** The "natural `ℝP¹`-structure" of `ℓ(p)` is read as the double
  covering `S(T_p HS²) → P(T_p HS²) ≅ ℝP¹`, through an oriented `θ : T_p HS² ≃ ℂ`
  (`GX.linkToRP1`). Evidence: l.902–905, where one turn of `ℓ(p)` is `δ²`; here `ℓ(p)` has lifted
  holonomy `δ²` (`GX.hasLiftedHolonomy_link`). It is the only reading uniform in `p`: at a
  space-like or light-like `p`, `Γ_p` fixes four or two rays of `ℓ(p)`, so no `Γ_p`-equivariant
  degree-one identification `ℓ(p) ≅ ℝP¹` exists (not formalized). At a time-like `p` the
  degree-one visual structure of (c) is an alternative.

